{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004606",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 6 candidates are direct matches to age-related macular degeneration (same EFO). Among them, PGS004606 reports the strongest and most relevant visible performance evidence in a large European evaluation (full_model AUROC=0.71; validation n=163,011; GWAS/EVAL EUR with PRS-CS), and uses a phenotype explicitly labeled age-related macular degeneration. Competing models either lack standard discrimination metrics (PGS001834/PGS002041 only provide partial-r), are tied to a different reported phenotype despite AMD labeling (PGS002269 uses retinal layer thickness as the reported phenotype), show weaker AUROC in the same evaluation setting (PGS004607 AUROC=0.65), or have higher reported AUROC but on a much smaller validation sample and a narrower outcome definition (PGS004952 late AMD; validation n=1,232) with an apparent formatting inconsistency in AUROC reporting (84.2 vs 0.842). Confidence is moderate because AUCs are not provided for several candidates, and PGS004952’s discrimination appears higher but is difficult to compare directly given small n and reporting ambiguity."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000119",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 20 candidates are direct matches to basal cell carcinoma (trait_efo/trait_reported align). Among models with reported discrimination, PGS000119 shows the highest AUROC in this context (0.64, 95% CI 0.62–0.66) with European evaluation ancestry, and an OR per SD of 1.65 (1.56–1.75). Close competitors include PGS000452/PGS000454 (AUROC 0.632) and PGS000730 (AUROC 0.624), but they do not exceed PGS000119 on AUROC in the provided metrics. Confidence is Moderate because several models have similar performance, many candidates lack complete comparable metrics (AUC/R2 missing), and some later-release scores (e.g., PGS003416/PGS003417) do not report AUROC here, limiting head-to-head comparison."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000137",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "There are multiple direct-match PRS candidates for glaucoma (15 total). PGS000137 is the most strongly supported by the visible performance evidence because it reports a comparatively high validated full-model AUROC of 0.80 in European ancestry (PPM000422) and is explicitly for glaucoma/POAG, whereas many other candidates either lack AUROC entirely (e.g., PGS000350, PGS000764, PGS002761), report lower full-model AUROC in Europeans (e.g., PGS001321/PGS001322/PGS001323 ~0.70–0.72; PGS001797 0.749; PGS004944 0.748; PGS002741 0.67), or provide non-AUROC metrics (partial-r, incremental R2) that are not directly comparable. However, this remains sub-optimal because the reported AUROC is for a covariate-adjusted full model (age/sex/family history) rather than clearly isolating PGS-only discrimination, and cross-candidate comparability is limited by heterogeneous phenotyping (glaucoma vs POAG vs self-reported), methods, and metric reporting."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001043",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Seven direct-match candidates are present for the target trait hyperthyroidism, spanning thyrotoxicosis/hyperthyroidism and Graves' disease. Among the hyperthyroidism-labeled models, PGS001043 is the most directly aligned in reported trait name (\"Hyperthyroidism, thyrotoxicosis\") versus PGS001042 which is explicitly a time-to-event thyrotoxicosis definition. PGS001043 and PGS001042 show very similar reported performance in European validation (full-model AUROC ~0.714 with similar incremental AUROC ~0.046), so the choice is mainly on trait-label alignment rather than a clear performance separation. Evidence remains limited/heterogeneous across candidates because several other direct matches (PGS001815/PGS002023) do not report AUROC/R2 (only partial-r), and Graves' disease scores (PGS005264-266) are related but not explicitly the overall hyperthyroidism phenotype; additionally covariates are unknown for the Graves models. Therefore a direct match exists but the best choice is not unequivocally established from the provided context."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004911",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches to hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM), but PGS004911 is best supported by the provided performance evidence: it reports the highest AUROC (0.8) and highest R² (0.048) among the PRS-CS models, with a large European evaluation sample size (n=343,182) and standard covariates (age, sex, PCs). PGS000739 shows higher AUROC (0.821) but that metric is for a full clinical model including multiple clinical risk factors and rare-variant carrier status, so it is not directly comparable as a genetic-score-only option from the visible metadata. PGS000778 lacks AUROC/R² and has a very small validation sample (n=368) in a specific carrier/event context. Remaining limitation: only one performance record is shown for each of PGS004910/PGS004911 and all reported evaluations are European, and the MTAG-based construction for PGS004911 may affect interpretability versus the non-MTAG HCM score, but within the provided context it has the strongest reported predictive metrics."
}

{
